Chapter: Lapic
  cites Foreword chapter Board
  grounds Device.Mmio

 The local APIC, as a driver a guest can call.

 The registers have been driven all along -- but from inside the
 COMPILER, by X86_64Boot, which writes them as machine code during SMP
 bring-up and arms each core's timer. That is emit-time code: it builds
 instructions, it does not execute them, and nothing a guest program
 could call reached the LAPIC at all. `vga-terminal-demo` cited this
 chapter and called lapic-drain against a chapter that had never been
 written; the cite is now an error rather than a silence, which is how
 the gap was finally legible.

 The base is 0xFEE00000. It is above the 3 GB RAM ceiling, in the device
 gigabyte the runtime page tables map present/RW/NX, so a load or store
 there is decoded by a device rather than by memory -- exactly as on real
 silicon. Under codex-vm the access leaves the guest as an MMIO exit and
 is served by the emulated LAPIC.

Section: Registers

 Offsets from the Intel SDM volume 3, table 10-1. These are the same
 numbers X86_64Boot carries for its own emission; the duplication is
 real but the two are not reachable from one another -- the compiler
 cannot cite the OS quire. If they drift, a guest and its own boot code
 disagree about the same chip.

  lapic-base : Integer = #FEE00000
  lapic-reg-id : Integer = #20
  lapic-reg-version : Integer = #30
  lapic-reg-eoi : Integer = #B0
  lapic-reg-sivr : Integer = #F0
  lapic-reg-isr : Integer = #100

 The in-service register is eight 32-bit words on a 16-byte stride,
 one bit per vector, 256 vectors in all.

  lapic-isr-stride : Integer = 16
  lapic-isr-words : Integer = 8
  lapic-bits-per-word : Integer = 32

Section: Reads

 The id sits in the top byte. codex-vm answers this per core, so an
 application processor and the boot processor give different answers --
 which is the only reason it is worth reading.

  lapic-id : [Device.Mmio] Integer
  lapic-id = act
    v <- mmio-read-32 (lapic-base + lapic-reg-id)
    bit-shru v 24
  end

 The version register is the honest liveness probe for this chapter. It
 is a fixed non-zero constant the hardware reports about itself, so a
 read that returns it proves the MMIO round trip reached a real decoder;
 a read that returns 0 is what a missing mapping, an unmapped BAR and a
 stub all return. Every other register here can read 0 legitimately.

  lapic-version : [Device.Mmio] Integer
  lapic-version = mmio-read-32 (lapic-base + lapic-reg-version)

 The spurious-interrupt vector register carries the vector the chip
 delivers when it has nothing better to say, and bit 8 is the software
 enable for the whole local APIC.

  lapic-sivr : [Device.Mmio] Integer
  lapic-sivr = mmio-read-32 (lapic-base + lapic-reg-sivr)

Section: Writes

 Writing the EOI register tells the local APIC the highest-priority
 interrupt in service is finished. The value is architecturally ignored;
 zero is what the SDM requires be written.

  lapic-eoi : [Device.Mmio] Integer
  lapic-eoi = mmio-write-32 (lapic-base + lapic-reg-eoi) 0

  lapic-set-sivr : Integer -> [Device.Mmio] Integer
  lapic-set-sivr (v) = mmio-write-32 (lapic-base + lapic-reg-sivr) v

Section: Drain

 Acknowledge every interrupt the local APIC still holds in service, and
 answer how many. A program that takes over a machine someone else
 booted -- firmware, a bootloader -- inherits whatever that owner left
 in service, and an un-acknowledged vector blocks every equal or lower
 priority interrupt forever after.

 The walk is over the ISR bitmap: eight words, thirty-two bits each, one
 EOI per set bit. It is bounded by construction at 256 acknowledgements
 and has no fuel parameter because it cannot spin -- each word is read
 once and each bit is examined once.

 What codex-vm proves about this is nothing. Its LAPIC models the id,
 the version, the SIVR, the ICR and the per-core timer, and returns 0
 for every other offset, the ISR included -- so under codex-vm the walk
 reads eight zero words, sends no EOI, and returns 0. That is a correct
 result on a machine with nothing in service, and it is indistinguishable
 from a driver that does not work. `codex/test/lapic-regs` therefore
 tests what the emulator can actually contradict and does not pretend
 the drain is covered.

  lapic-drain : [Device.Mmio] Integer
  lapic-drain = lapic-drain-word 0 0

  lapic-drain-word : Integer, Integer -> [Device.Mmio] Integer
  lapic-drain-word (i) (acc) =
    if i >= lapic-isr-words then acc
    else lapic-drain-word-at i acc

  lapic-drain-word-at : Integer, Integer -> [Device.Mmio] Integer
  lapic-drain-word-at (i) (acc) = act
    w <- mmio-read-32 (lapic-base + lapic-reg-isr + i * lapic-isr-stride)
    n <- lapic-drain-bit w 0 0
    lapic-drain-word (i + 1) (acc + n)
  end

  lapic-drain-bit : Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Mmio] Integer
  lapic-drain-bit (w) (b) (acc) =
    if b >= lapic-bits-per-word then acc
    else if bit-and (bit-shru w b) 1 == 0 then lapic-drain-bit w (b + 1) acc
    else lapic-drain-bit-ack w b acc

  lapic-drain-bit-ack : Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Mmio] Integer
  lapic-drain-bit-ack (w) (b) (acc) = act
    e <- lapic-eoi
    lapic-drain-bit w (b + 1) (acc + 1)
  end
